{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"},{"text":"Good question, and it exposes a wrong premise worth correcting: **we don't fold in curry at all**, and we don't fold in eval-path either. The fold lives in exactly two spots:\n\n- `assemble_arg_tree` (client) — line 2380\n- `run_image` (server) — line 797\n\nCurry (`caos_curry`/`curry_from_entries`) doesn't fold. And eval-path (`eval_command`) currently calls `assemble_arg_tree` with an **empty** store, so it doesn't fold there yet either.\n\n## Why those two, and not \"eval-path\"\n\nThe thing that gets marked and matched is the **full assembled arg tree** (`{image, worker1, std, salt, …}`), not the image. eval-path's image step (`eval_std_entry`) returns *just the image* — before the run's call-args are added — so it's too early: a path-only reader like `std/github-push` resolves to `{image: bash, worker1: push-script}`, and you can't check that superset until `worker1` is present, i.e. at assembly.\n\nSo the real hook is \"**wherever an expression becomes a runnable arg tree**,\" and there are two such assemblers:\n\n- **`assemble_arg_tree`** is the client-side one. Crucially, eval-path's `run` verb *calls it* — so \"fold at assemble\" already *is* \"fold when eval-path forms a run tree.\" It's not a competing location; it's eval-path's constructor. (It's also reached by a direct `caos-cli run <expr>`, which never goes through eval-path — another reason it can't move up into eval-path.)\n- **`run_image`** is the server-side twin. This is the one that can't be eval-path: a worker doesn't call eval-path to run a sub-worker — it emits a **continuation** that the *server* resolves, assembling the sub-run's arg tree itself (byte-identically to the client). The common secret case — `deploy` run-thens `github-push` — is assembled here. If we folded only client-side, that leaf would never get the mark and never be injected.\n\nSo it's not \"run + curry + eval-path\" — it's \"the client arg-tree assembler\" + \"its server twin,\" which is the same duplication every arg-tree construction already has (the server rebuilds trees to match client hashes).\n\n## Where curry *does* come in\n\nNot folding in curry is exactly why **callers aren't isolated yet**. Currying `github-push` into `deploy` produces a curry node with no mark, so `deploy`'s input is identical across users. Marking an *embeddable* curried/eval'd worker — folding at curry (and threading the store into eval-path so its `run`/`curry` results fold) — is precisely the deferred **caller-propagation** work. So your instinct (\"fold in eval-path, including curry\") is pointing at the fix for the caller leak; it's an *addition*, not something we do today.\n\nNet: today's two folds are correct and necessary for the *run/leaf* cases (a runnable tree is assembled in exactly those two places). The move you're imagining — fold as expressions form embeddable trees (curry + eval-path) — is the next step for isolating callers, and it *adds* hooks rather than replacing the run/`run_image` ones (the server must still fold for sub-runs regardless).\n\nWant me to take that on: thread the store into eval-path and fold at curry, so embedded/eval'd workers carry the mark and callers get isolated?","type":"text"}],"results":[{"content":[{"text":"exit: 0\nstdout:\n=== client fold call sites (client_secret_hash) ===\n2380:    if let Some(hash) = client_secret_hash(store, &base)? {\n3219:fn client_secret_hash(\n=== server fold/check (secret_hash / grant) ===\n340:        let granted = crate::secrets::grant(secrets, &arg_entries);\n797:    if let Some(hash) = crate::secrets::secret_hash(secrets, &base) {\n=== curry fns ===\n2880:pub fn caos_curry(t: &dyn Transport, arg_tree: &str, rest: &[String]) -> Result<(), String> {\n2892:pub fn cli_curry(t: &dyn Transport, arg_tree: &str, rest: &[String]) -> Result<(), String> {\n2948:fn curry_from_entries(\n\nstderr:\n","type":"text"}],"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T79sXaDet2hWm5fRdrokpY","type":